## Working agreements

- Tests first for any new behavior; extend the suite before the feature.
- Plan mode for any change touching more than one file. Wait for approval.
- Small commits, one logical change each. Never commit on red tests — the
  hooks enforce this; do not disable them.
- If a [LOCKED] spec blocks a task, stop and say so rather than work around it.
- Make source changes with the Edit/Write file tools, never with sed, perl,
  heredocs or scripts through Bash. The verification gate only sees file
  tool edits.

## If verification fails

1. The gate feeds the failure straight back — fix it and retry. This is the
   normal loop, not an exception.
2. Stuck or looping on the same failure? `/rewind` restores files and
   conversation to before the bad edit (edits are checkpointed
   automatically); take a different approach from there.
3. Honest limit: the Stop gate blocks once per stop (the anti-loop
   `stop_hook_active` pattern) — a second consecutive stop is NOT re-gated,
   so a red suite can still be handed back. Treat any Stop-gate failure text
   as unfinished work, never as a formality.
4. Known blind spot: edits made through Bash (`sed -i`, heredocs, scripts)
   never trigger the edit gate — only Write/Edit tool calls do. The Bash
   guard notices the common shapes and re-runs the gate on the touched
   file, but it is a net, not the path. Make source changes with the
   Edit/Write file tools, never with sed, perl, heredocs or scripts
   through Bash.
5. `[LOOP DETECTED]` means the same failure blocked three edits in a row.
   Do not try a fourth fix. Stop, and report what was tried and why each
   attempt failed; the operator decides whether to rewind.

## Commands

- Test: `uv run pytest -q`
- Lint: `uv run ruff check .` / format: `uv run ruff format .`
- Types: `uv run mypy`
